    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Pompeii grew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> villages of the 8th century BCE into a walled town that passed under Greek, Etruscan, Samnite and then Roman influence, and became a Roman colony after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> besieged it in 89 BCE.</li><li>A severe earthquake struck on 5 February 62, and the town was still being rebuilt when the eruption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a> in 79 buried it under ash and pumice.</li><li>The ruins stayed largely undisturbed until the late 16th century, and excavation since the mid-18th century has made Pompeii a main source for the study of Roman life and urban planning.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Giuseppe Fiorelli realised in 1863 that voids in the ash were spaces left by decomposed bodies, and devised the technique of injecting plaster into them to recreate the forms of the victims.</p></aside>
    </section>

