    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">8th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscans</a> found five villages on the site</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">474 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Cumae, allied with Syracuse, defeats the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> and gains control of the area</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">89 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> besieges Pompeii, which becomes a Roman colony</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">70 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Amphitheatre of Pompeii is built</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">62</span><span class="rf-text">A severe earthquake damages the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">79</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a> erupts and buries Pompeii</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1748</span><span class="rf-text">Excavations begin on the site</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1863</span><span class="rf-text">Giuseppe Fiorelli takes charge and introduces plaster casts and scientific documentation</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/stabiae"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stabiae.jpg" alt="Stabiae" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/stabiae">Stabiae</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wall_painting_from_Stabiae,_1st_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Pompeii was built about 40 m above sea level on a coastal lava plateau formed by earlier eruptions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a>, 8 km away. The plateau fell steeply to the south and partly to the west into the sea. The city once stood by the shoreline and is today about 700 m inland. The mouth of the navigable Sarno river, beside the city, was protected by lagoons and served early Greek and Phoenician sailors as a haven port, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> later developed. According to Theodor Kraus, the name may come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> word for the number five, pompe, which would suggest that the community consisted of five hamlets, or that it was settled by a family group, the gens Pompeia.</p><p>The first stable settlements on the site date to the 8th century BCE, when the Oscans, a population of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founded five villages in the area. From around 740 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> and Pompeii entered their orbit. The Doric Temple, the most important building of this period, stood away from the centre, in what later became the Triangular Forum, and the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> was introduced. In the early 6th century BCE the villages merged into a single community at the crossroad between Cumae, Nola and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stabiae">Stabiae</a>, surrounded by a tufa wall that enclosed much more than the early town, including agricultural land. A small port was built near the river mouth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hannibal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hannibal.jpg" alt="Hannibal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hannibal_Barca_bust_from_Capua_photo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fratelli Alinari, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The city grew in stages. Its earliest part lay in regions VII and VIII, with an irregular street plan, and expansion from this old town accelerated after 450 BCE. In the late 4th century BCE the city began to spread from its nucleus into the open area inside the walls, with a more regular street plan. The walls were reinforced in the early 3rd century BCE, and again after the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> in 218 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s invasion threatened many cities. Pompeii continued to flourish through the production and trade of wine and oil with places such as Provence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and through intensive agriculture on farms around the city.</p><p>In the 2nd century BCE it enriched itself by taking part in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>'s conquest of the east. A statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> in the Forum was erected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-mummius-achaicus">Lucius Mummius</a> in gratitude for the city's support in the sack of Corinth. These riches let Pompeii expand to its ultimate limits, and the Forum and many public and private buildings were built, among them the Large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a>, the Temple of Jupiter, the Basilica, the Stabian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Baths</a> and a two-storey portico. By the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>'s conquest in 89 BCE the street layout was largely complete and most of the insulae were built. The city covered 64 to 67 hectares. Its population in 79 CE is put at 20,000 by one estimate, while household counts give 11,000 to 11,500.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sulla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulla.jpg" alt="Sulla" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Q._Pompeius_Rufus,_denarius,_54_BC,_RRC_434-1_(Sulla_only).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNG, CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By 524 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a> had settled in the area, including Pompeii, which became a member of the Etruscan League of cities. Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, they controlled the city without conquering it militarily, and Pompeii enjoyed a sort of autonomy. In 474 BCE Cumae, allied with Syracuse, defeated the Etruscans at the Battle of Cumae and gained control of the area. The Samnites, a people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molise">Molise</a> and allies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, conquered Greek Cumae between 423 and 420 BCE, and it is likely that the surrounding territory, Pompeii included, had been conquered around 424 BCE. The new rulers gradually imposed their architecture and enlarged the town.</p><p>From 343 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samnite-wars">Samnite Wars</a> brought a Roman army into the Campanian plain. Although governed by the Samnites, Pompeii entered the Roman orbit and stayed faithful to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> through the third Samnite war and the war against Pyrrhus. After the Samnite Wars, from 290 BCE, it had to accept the status of socii of Rome, keeping its own language and administration. Pompeii rebelled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social Wars</a>, and in 89 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> besieged it. It surrendered after the conquest of Nola and became a Roman colony named Colonia Cornelia Veneria Pompeianorum. Sulla's veterans were given land and property, many who had opposed Rome were dispossessed, and the Pompeians were granted Roman citizenship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the conquest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> became the main language of the city, and many old aristocratic families Latinised their names as a sign of assimilation. The graffiti on outside walls and inside rooms give many examples of the colloquial Vulgar Latin spoken at the time, in contrast to the formal language of classical writers. Excavation has uncovered hundreds of private homes and businesses reflecting various architectural styles and social classes, and luxurious houses with lavish decoration. Among them are the House of the Faun, the House of the Vettii and the House of Menander, and outside the walls the Villa of the Mysteries.</p><p>The public buildings included the Forum, two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a>, at least four public baths and the Amphitheatre, which scholars have cited as a model of crowd control. An aqueduct, a branch of the Serino <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">Aqueduct</a>, supplied the public baths, more than 25 street fountains and many private houses and businesses. There were at least 31 bakeries, nearly 100 thermopolia that sold hot food and drink, and workshops for wool, among them fulleries and the Building of Eumachia, which was the wool market or the seat of the fullers' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>. A garum workshop made fish sauce. Wine was exported, and barley, wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olive-oil">olive oil</a> were also produced. Carbonised remains show that people ate emmer wheat, chickpeas, figs, olives, grapes, walnuts and many other plants.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Pompeii&#8221; cites where it covers Agriculture and horticulture:</p><ul><li>Frederick G. Meyer, The natural history of Pompeii (Cambridge University Press, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a>'s siege of 89 BCE left its traces in the walls. He targeted the vulnerable Porta Ercolano with artillery, and the impact craters of thousands of ballista shots can still be seen. Many nearby buildings inside the walls were destroyed. In 59 CE a serious riot and bloodshed in the amphitheatre between Pompeians and Nucerians, recorded in a fresco, led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a> to send the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a> and to ban further events for ten years.</p><p>On 5 February 62 a severe earthquake did considerable damage around the bay, particularly to Pompeii, and is believed to have registered between 5 and 6 on the Richter scale. Fires caused by fallen oil lamps added to the panic. Between 62 and 79 most rebuilding was done in the private sector, and some public buildings, such as the Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Baths</a>, were begun after the earthquake. The city was still flourishing.</p><p>In 79 CE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a> erupted, in one of the deadliest eruptions in history, which lasted two days. A rain of pumice lasted about 18 hours and allowed most inhabitants to escape; only about 1,150 bodies have been found on the site. Then pyroclastic flows struck, incinerating or suffocating those who remained. A study published in 2010 indicates that heat was the main cause of death. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-younger">Pliny the Younger</a> described the eruption from Misenum; his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> died trying to rescue victims. The date is disputed: one version of Pliny's letter gives August, another as late as 23 November, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a> inscription found in 2018 gives 17 October, and a study of 2022 determined 24 to 25 October.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/titus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/titus.jpg" alt="Titus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titus_Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek_IN3159.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus">Titus</a> appointed two ex-consuls to organise relief and gave large sums from the imperial treasury, but no work was done on recovery. Survivors and possibly thieves salvaged valuables, and the site was reoccupied as a small informal settlement that lasted until the fifth century. The name and location were forgotten, though Pompeii appears on the Tabula Peutingeriana of the 4th century. Further eruptions in 471 to 473 and in 512 covered the remains more deeply, and the area became known as La Civita.</p><p>In 1592 the architect Domenico Fontana ran into painted walls while digging an aqueduct, but kept the find secret. Excavations at the site began in 1748 under Roque Joaquin de Alcubierre, and an inscription found on 20 August 1763 identified the city as Pompeii. Karl Weber directed the first scientific excavations. Under the French, who ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> from 1806 to 1815, up to 700 workers were employed. Giuseppe Fiorelli took charge in 1863, devised the plaster casts and divided the city into nine regiones and its blocks into insulae. In the 1920s Amedeo Maiuri dug below the layers of 79 for the first time, and by 1960 most of Pompeii had been uncovered. The Allied bombing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and the earthquake of 1980 caused further damage.</p><p>Pompeii is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. About two thirds of the city has been excavated, and work now concentrates on documenting the ruins and slowing their decay, though new areas have been explored since 2018.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Pompeii was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Bay of Naples</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscans</a> in the 8th century BCE and ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruscans">Etruscans</a>, Samnites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a> buried it in 79 CE. Its ruins, excavated since the mid-18th century, show Roman life.</em></p>

